{{also|Mingle}}
==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|enm|mingil}}, {{m|en|mengle}}, from {{inh|en|enm|menglen}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|ming|le}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|mengen||to mingle, mix}}, {{cog|de|mengen||to mingle, mix}}. More at {{l|en|ming}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɪŋ.ɡəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-mingle.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋɡəl|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[intermix]]; to [[combine]] or [[join]], as an individual or part, with other parts, but commonly so as to be distinguishable in the product
#: {{syn|en|confuse|confound}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|book=Exodus|chapter=9|verse=24|passage=There was {{...}} fire '''mingled''' with the hail.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Martin Farquhar Tupper|chapter=Of Searching for Pride|title=Proverbial Philosophy: A Book of Thoughts and Arguments, Originally Treated|location=London|publisher=Joseph Rickerby,{{nb...|Sherbourn Lane, King William Street, City.}}|year=1838|page=69|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=K-g_AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA69|oclc=36892655|passage=Be aware of the smiling enemy, that openly sheatheth his weapon, / But '''mingleth''' poison in secret with the sacred salt of hospitality.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 11, 2006|work=New York Times|author=Sheryl Gay Stolberg|title=Bush Mourns 9/11 at Ground Zero as N.Y. Remembers|passage=Across the city yesterday, there was a feeling of bittersweet reunion as streams of humanity converged and mingled at dozens of memorial services.|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/11/nyregion/nyregionspecial3/11bush.html?hp&ex=1158033600&en=e468f88da52557ed&ei=5094&partner=homepage}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To associate or unite in a figurative way, or by ties of relationship
# to cause or allow to intermarry
# to intermarry.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ezra|9|2|passage=The holy seed have '''mingled''' themselves with the people of those lands.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To deprive of purity by mixture; to [[contaminate]].
#* '''a. 1729''', {{w|John Rogers (priest)|John Rogers}}, ''The Necessity of Universal Obedience''
#*: a '''mingled''', imperfect virtue
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make or prepare by mixing the ingredients of.
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Scarlet Letter|4|passage=[He] proceeded to '''mingle''' another draught.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To put together; to join.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|1|6|passage=The best feather of our wing have '''mingled''' sums <br>To buy a present for the emperor}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become mixed or blended.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To socialize with different people at a social event.
#* '''2009''', Jane Buckingham, ''The Modern Girl's Guide to Life''
#*:And allow a bit of a cocktail hour before the meal so that when your guests arrive, you have time to '''mingle''' before you step into the kitchen.

